    <Placemark>
      <name>Tel Arad (תל ערד)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Arad (תל ערד)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 05. Early Bronze Age (EB) (-3300 to -2200 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Deep Well</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Negev</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.5.2, 2.5.11, 2.5 (Pages 55, 56, 60, 61, 62, 63, 64, 65, 70, 71)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (05. Early Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>A well is located in the heart of the Early Bronze Age city. Although re-used in the Iron Age, it is suggested that its initial excavation dates to the Early Bronze Age as part of a larger runoff water collection system. An open-air pool/reservoir in the city center, formed by collecting rainwater runoff through radial streets into a natural depression, covering about one dunam. Water collection from rooftops into large pithoi is also suggested for this EB city. Arad had central water pools for collecting runoff, and large storage jars (pithoi) were used within houses for water storage. Clayey soil was used as a hydraulic plaster for water installations.</p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#style_Early_Bronze</styleUrl>
      <ExtendedData>
        <Data name="Site_Name"><value>Tel Arad</value></Data>
        <Data name="Hebrew_Name"><value>תל ערד</value></Data>
        <Data name="Era"><value>05. Early Bronze Age (EB) (-3300 to -2200 BCE)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Installation_Type"><value>Deep Well</value></Data>
        <Data name="Region"><value>Negev</value></Data>
        <Data name="Location_Precision"><value>Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dates"><value>-3300 to -2200 BCE</value></Data>
        <Data name="Chapter_Section"><value>2.5.2, 2.5.11, 2.5</value></Data>
        <Data name="Dissertation_Pages"><value>55, 56, 60, 61, 62, 63, 64, 65, 70, 71</value></Data>
        <Data name="Description"><value>A well is located in the heart of the Early Bronze Age city. Although re-used in the Iron Age, it is suggested that its initial excavation dates to the Early Bronze Age as part of a larger runoff water collection system. An open-air pool/reservoir in the city center, formed by collecting rainwater runoff through radial streets into a natural depression, covering about one dunam. Water collection from rooftops into large pithoi is also suggested for this EB city. Arad had central water pools for collecting runoff, and large storage jars (pithoi) were used within houses for water storage. Clayey soil was used as a hydraulic plaster for water installations.</value></Data>
      </ExtendedData>
      <Point>
        <coordinates>35.12535,31.28173,0</coordinates>
      </Point>
    </Placemark>
    <Placemark>
      <name>Tel Bet Yerah (בית ירח)</name>
      <description><![CDATA[<h3>Tel Bet Yerah (בית ירח)</h3>
<p><b>Period:</b> 05. Early Bronze Age (EB) (-3300 to -2200 BCE)</p>
<p><b>Installation Type:</b> Aqueduct Channel</p>
<p><b>Location Precision:</b> Tier 1: High Precision (Pinpoint Well/Installation &lt; 50m)</p>
<p><b>Region:</b> Jordan Valley</p>
<p><b>Dissertation Reference:</b> §2.5.17 (Pages 70)</p>
<hr/>
<p><b>Archaeological Description (05. Early Bronze Age):</b></p>
<p>A basalt-lined tunnel, roofed with basalt slabs, was discovered on the southern edge of Tel Bet Yerah. It is interpreted as part of an Early Bronze Age III water system, possibly an aqueduct bringing water from mountain springs or a channel collecting runoff within the city.</p>]]></description>
      <styleUrl>#style_Early_Bronze</styleUrl>
      <ExtendedData>
